<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Right Triangle Area From an Angle</h2>
  <p>Suppose a right triangle has a leg of <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, and the acute angle between those two sides measures about <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Its area follows directly: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times\\sin(36.87^\\circ)}{2}]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)\\approx0.6]]')} />, the area is approximately <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The triangle encloses 6 square metres of flat surface.</p>
  <p>This calculator handles that situation: with one leg, the hypotenuse, and the acute angle between them, sine turns the hypotenuse into the missing perpendicular height, and half of base times height gives the area in square units. The included angle must sit between the two sides entered, and each mode pairs one specific angle with its two sides.</p>
  <p>The angle <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} /> is rounded from about <span set:html={rich('[[36.8699^\\circ]]')} />; keeping full precision on the calculator gives the most accurate result.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the geometry -->
<section id="sec-geometry" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Sides and Angles of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>This guide uses one consistent notation throughout: <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> are the two acute angles, <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> is the right angle, side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> lies opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> lies opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area.</p>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one interior angle of exactly <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. Its two legs meet at that right angle, and the hypotenuse lies opposite it. The hypotenuse is always the longest side. The remaining two angles are acute, and because the three interior angles add to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B+C=180^\\circ]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} />, the acute angles satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />: knowing one tells you the other.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, labelled <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> in both calculator modes. To find it in a triangle drawn in any orientation, look for the side that does not touch the square corner marker: that side is the hypotenuse. It is longer than either leg, because it spans the whole right angle while each leg spans only an acute angle. Every area formula on this page uses <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> as one of its two sides, so identifying it correctly is the first skill to practise.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent leg is the leg that touches the chosen reference angle without being the hypotenuse. For angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> the adjacent leg is <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />; for angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> the adjacent leg is <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Name the reference angle first, then read off its adjacent leg. Swapping the reference angle silently swaps which leg counts as adjacent, and that swap is behind many of the common mistakes listed later on this page.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side lies directly across from the chosen angle, not touching it. For angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is opposite; for angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is opposite. The labels change with the reference angle, but the hypotenuse stays <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> in both cases. Holding the reference angle fixed while you label the triangle keeps every later step consistent.</p>

  <h3>What Is an Included Angle?</h3>
  <p>An included angle is the angle sitting between two specified sides. Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is included between sides <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />; angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> is included between sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. The general sine area formula only works when the angle is the one included between the two sides used, because the formula reads the perpendicular height off that angle. Pairing a side with an angle it does not touch breaks the geometry the formula depends on.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('angle-anatomy-modes.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC showing both area modes: angle A between sides b and c, angle B between sides a and c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Both calculator modes use the same triangle. Mode 1 pairs side b, hypotenuse c, and the included angle A; Mode 2 pairs side a, hypotenuse c, and the included angle B.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Why sine appears in the formula -->
<section id="sec-sine" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Area Formula Use Sine?</h2>
  <p>Both calculator modes rest on one general formula for the area of any triangle given two sides and their included angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac12 xy\\sin(\\theta)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> are two side lengths, <span set:html={rich('[[\\theta]]')} /> is the angle included between them, and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area. The sine term is what builds the perpendicular height out of the second side.</p>

  <h3>How the General Formula Works</h3>
  <p>The familiar triangle area rule is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12\\times\\text{base}\\times\\text{height}]]')} />. When the perpendicular height is not measured directly, sine can recover it from another side. Take a base of length <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and a second side of length <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> meeting it at an angle <span set:html={rich('[[\\theta]]')} />. The perpendicular component of that second side, the height that counts for area, is <span set:html={rich('[[h=y\\sin(\\theta)]]')} />. Substituting into the base-height rule gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 x(y\\sin(\\theta))]]')} />, the general formula above. The included angle must correspond to the two selected sides; an angle from elsewhere in the triangle measures a different perpendicular.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Area Divided by Two?</h3>
  <p>The triangle occupies half the area of the parallelogram built on the same base with the same perpendicular height. With base <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />, the product <span set:html={rich('[[x\\times h]]')} /> is the full parallelogram, and the triangle is exactly half of it. Multiplying the two sides and the sine value without the halving therefore produces twice the desired area.</p>

  <h3>Why the Hypotenuse Can Be Used</h3>
  <p>One of the two sides in the included-angle formula can be the hypotenuse. Paired with the correct leg and the acute angle between them, the hypotenuse carries enough information to build the area: its sine component is precisely the perpendicular height relative to that leg as base. This is why the calculator never asks for the second leg. The hypotenuse is not a special exception to the formula; it is an ordinary choice of side, sitting at an ordinary included angle.</p>

  <h3>Why the Angle Must Be Correct</h3>
  <p>Replacing angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> with angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> while keeping the same side pairing usually gives the wrong result. Take the 3-4-5 triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. The correct Mode 1 calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times\\sin(36.87^\\circ))/2\\approx6]]')} />. Substituting the other angle instead gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times\\sin(53.13^\\circ))/2\\approx8]]')} />, because <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(53.13^\\circ)\\approx0.8]]')} />. The answer 8 is not a rounding artifact; it is the area of a different configuration, one where the side pairing no longer matches the included angle. Getting the angle-side pairing right is one of this page's most important lessons.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('sine-height.svg')} alt="3-4-5 right triangle showing a equals c times sin A equals 3 as the perpendicular height for area" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Sine of angle A turns the hypotenuse into the perpendicular height a = 3, so the familiar K = ba/2 and the direct formula K = bc sin(A)/2 agree.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Method one -->
<section id="sec-mode1" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Area Using Side b, Hypotenuse c, and Angle A</h2>
  <p>Mode 1 of the calculator uses:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The variables are: <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> the leg adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> the acute included angle between <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> the area.</p>

  <h3>Step-by-Step Formula Derivation</h3>
  <p>Start from the legs area formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} />. From the sine ratio at angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\sin(A)]]')} />. The opposite leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is being rewritten as the sine component of the hypotenuse. Substituting gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{b(c\\sin(A))}{2}]]')} />, and rearranging yields <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}}]]]')} />. The unknown opposite leg never needs to be entered because the formula constructs it from the hypotenuse and the angle behind the scenes.</p>

  <h3>Complete Worked Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Write the formula.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=bc\\sin(A)/2]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Substitute.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times\\sin(36.87^\\circ))/2]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate the sine.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)\\approx0.6]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Multiply.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K\\approx(20\\times0.6)/2]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Divide.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K\\approx6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The answer says the triangle covers about 6 square metres. Because the input angle is rounded, the word approximately belongs in the report, though with full precision the same inputs give 6 exactly.</p>

  <h3>Verify Using the Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>The missing leg follows from the Pythagorean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{25-16}=3]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} />. Both routes agree because they describe the same triangle: the sine route builds the missing leg from the angle, while the legs route uses the leg directly.</p>

  <h3>When Should You Use Mode 1?</h3>
  <p>Use Mode 1 when the known measurements are the leg adjacent to the known acute angle, the hypotenuse, and that angle itself. If both legs are already known, the direct legs formula is simpler and skips the angle entirely. Mode 1 is for the common field situation where an angle and two lengths come from the same measurement setup, and the second leg was never recorded.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Method two -->
<section id="sec-mode2" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Area Using Side a, Hypotenuse c, and Angle B</h2>
  <p>Mode 2 of the calculator uses:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ac\\sin(B)}{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The variables are: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> the leg adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, which is opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> the hypotenuse; <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> the included acute angle between <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />; and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> the area.</p>

  <h3>Step-by-Step Formula Derivation</h3>
  <p>Start again from <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} />. The sine ratio measured at angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> reads <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(B)=b/c]]')} />, because from <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />'s viewpoint the opposite side is <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Hence <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\sin(B)]]')} />. Substituting gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{a(c\\sin(B))}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ac\\sin(B)}{2}}]]]')} />. The formula uses side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> even though <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is called the opposite side in the page's main notation; the label opposite belongs to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, while relative to angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> the same side is adjacent. Side labels are always relative to the chosen reference angle.</p>

  <h3>Complete Worked Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Write the formula.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=ac\\sin(B)/2]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Substitute.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times5\\times\\sin(53.13^\\circ))/2]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate the sine.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(53.13^\\circ)\\approx0.8]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Multiply and divide.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K\\approx6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The result is the same 6 square metres as the Mode 1 example, because both examples describe the same 3-4-5 triangle viewed from its two acute angles.</p>

  <h3>Verify Using the Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{c^2-a^2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{25-9}=4]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} />. The agreement is expected: the sine formula quietly reconstructed the missing leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> from the hypotenuse and the angle.</p>

  <h3>When Should You Use Mode 2?</h3>
  <p>Use Mode 2 when the known side is <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is known, and the measured angle is the one touching side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, namely angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. The two modes are not interchangeable without also changing the side and angle labels; feeding Mode 2 the inputs of Mode 1 computes the area of a different triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Why both formulas produce the same area -->
<section id="sec-same" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Two Angle-Based Formulas, One Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Both modes calculate the area of the same geometric object, so they must agree. For Mode 1, <span set:html={rich('[[K=bc\\sin(A)/2]]')} />, and since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /> for a valid right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[bc\\sin(A)=ab]]')} />. For Mode 2, <span set:html={rich('[[K=ac\\sin(B)/2]]')} />, and since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(B)=b/c]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[ac\\sin(B)=ab]]')} />. Both formulas simplify to <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />, the familiar legs formula. The two modes are two different input routes to one quantity.</p>

  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Method</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Inputs</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Legs formula</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4]]')} /></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">6 square units</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Mode 1</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[b=4,\\;c=5,\\;A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} /></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Approximately 6</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Mode 2</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;c=5,\\;B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} /></td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Approximately 6</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Rounded angle measurements can introduce small numerical differences between the two modes, because <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} /> is only an approximation of the true <span set:html={rich('[[36.8699^\\circ]]')} />. A discrepancy of a few hundredths is a rounding effect, not a disagreement between the formulas.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('mode1-vs-mode2.svg')} alt="Same 3-4-5 triangle solved by Mode 1 and Mode 2 formulas, both giving area 6, verified by legs formula" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Two included angles, two equivalent formulas, one triangle area.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Angle Calculator</h2>
  <p>This walkthrough follows the actual calculator interface on this page, step by step.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the measurements already known.</strong> Confirm you have one leg, the hypotenuse, and an acute angle from the same triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose the correct mode.</strong> Select Mode 1 for side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; select Mode 2 for side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm which acute angle is being measured.</strong> The angle must be one of the two acute angles, not the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the corresponding leg.</strong> The leg must touch the measured angle; check the adjacent and opposite definitions above if unsure.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the leg length.</strong> Type the measured leg into the leg field of the selected mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse length.</strong> Type it into the hypotenuse field; it must be the longest of the two lengths.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible measurement units.</strong> Both lengths must use the same unit before calculating.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the acute angle in degrees.</strong> The calculator expects degrees, not radians.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that the angle is strictly between <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />.</strong> Boundary values describe degenerate shapes, not triangles.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that the hypotenuse is longer than the leg.</strong> A leg equal to or longer than the hypotenuse cannot belong to a right triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the leg, hypotenuse, and angle describe the same right triangle.</strong> The consistency tests in the validation section show how.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong> The result appears with the solution steps shown.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the result and solution steps.</strong> Trace each substitution to make sure the intended inputs were used.</li>
    <li><strong>Interpret the result in square units.</strong> The area unit is the square of the chosen length unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset to clear the previous calculation.</strong> This empties all fields before the next triangle is entered.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Notice that each mode asks for a different leg and a different angle. The modes are distinct because the included-angle pairing is different, not because one mode is a shortcut of the other.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: Four foundational examples -->
<section id="sec-foundations" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Right Triangle Area From an Angle</h2>
  <p>Four examples build the method on familiar triangles, from the 3-4-5 triple to special-angle cases.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: 3-4-5 Triangle Using Angle A</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />, use <span set:html={rich('[[K=bc\\sin(A)/2]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times\\sin(36.87^\\circ))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K\\approx(20\\times0.6)/2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K\\approx6}]]]')} />. The area is 6 square units because the triangle encloses six unit squares of flat surface; with full-precision angle the value is exactly 6.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Same Triangle Using Angle B</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />, use <span set:html={rich('[[K=ac\\sin(B)/2]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times5\\times\\sin(53.13^\\circ))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K\\approx(15\\times0.8)/2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K\\approx6}]]]')} />. The result matches Example 1 because both examples describe the same triangle; the two angles are complementary views of one shape.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />. The leg follows from <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(30^\\circ)=5\\sqrt{3}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\sqrt{3})(10)\\sin(30^\\circ)/2]]')} />, and since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30^\\circ)=1/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\approx21.65}]]]')} />. Special-angle values such as <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30^\\circ)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(30^\\circ)]]')} /> give exact forms before any decimal rounding, which is why these triangles are useful for checking the calculator by hand.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} />, the legs are equal: <span set:html={rich('[[a=b=5\\sqrt{2}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\sqrt{2})(10)\\sin(45^\\circ)/2]]')} />, and since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(45^\\circ)=\\sqrt{2}/2]]')} />, the product simplifies to <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=25}]]]')} />. This is an isosceles right triangle, and as shown later, it also has the maximum area possible for a right triangle with a hypotenuse of 10 units.</p>
</section>

