<!-- Section 10: Consistency of inputs -->
<section id="sec-consistency" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether the Side Lengths and Angle Form a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>A leg, a hypotenuse, and an acute angle carry more information than a right triangle needs: two of the three determine the third, so all three must agree. The checks below confirm the computed number describes a right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Validating Mode 1</h3>
  <p>For Mode 1 the consistency relation is <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} />, equivalently <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=c\\cos(A)}]]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=0.8]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. An arbitrary angle such as <span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /> would not describe the same right triangle with these side lengths: <span set:html={rich('[[5\\cos(60^\\circ)=2.5\\neq4]]')} />, so the three inputs would be mutually inconsistent.</p>

  <h3>Validating Mode 2</h3>
  <p>For Mode 2 the relation is <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(B)=a/c]]')} />, equivalently <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=c\\cos(B)}]]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B=\\cos^{-1}(0.6)\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. The chosen angle must correspond to the actual measurements; a leg of 3 with a hypotenuse of 5 simply does not meet at an included angle of, say, <span set:html={rich('[[70^\\circ]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Why the General Area Formula Can Still Return a Number</h3>
  <p>The formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12xy\\sin(\\theta)]]')} /> computes the area of a general triangle defined by two sides and their included angle. An incompatible angle-side combination still returns a number, but the resulting triangle is not right-angled: it is some other triangle that happens to share those three inputs. The arithmetic result does not by itself prove the inputs describe a valid right triangle; the cosine check above does that work.</p>

  <h3>How to Handle Rounded Angle Measurements</h3>
  <p>An angle such as <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} /> may be a rounded display of approximately <span set:html={rich('[[36.8699^\\circ]]')} />. Small mismatches from display or measurement rounding should be distinguished from genuinely inconsistent geometry: a few hundredths of a degree of slack is rounding, while several degrees of mismatch means the inputs disagree. No numerical validation tolerance is claimed for the calculator itself; the distinction is a judgement made from the measurements at hand.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Alternative formulas -->
<section id="sec-alternatives" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Alternative Ways to Find Right Triangle Area Using Angles</h2>
  <p>These are mathematical alternatives, not extra calculator modes; they show the same geometry under other input combinations.</p>

  <h3>Using the Hypotenuse and One Acute Angle</h3>
  <p>For a valid right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\sin(A)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} />. Substituting into <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{c^2\\sin(A)\\cos(A)}{2}}]]]')} />. With the double-angle identity <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(2A)=2\\sin(A)\\cos(A)]]')} />, this becomes <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{c^2\\sin(2A)}{4}}]]]')} />. The hypotenuse and one acute angle determine the triangle completely, so no leg measurement is needed at all.</p>

  <h3>Using One Leg and Its Adjacent Angle</h3>
  <p>If side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> are known, then <span set:html={rich('[[a=b\\tan(A)]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{b^2\\tan(A)}{2}}]]]')} />. The pairing matters: <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> must be the leg adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> for the tangent to produce the opposite leg.</p>

  <h3>Using Side a and Angle B</h3>
  <p>Mirroring the previous case for angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b=a\\tan(B)]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{a^2\\tan(B)}{2}}]]]')} />. Side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, which is exactly why the tangent again recovers the opposite leg.</p>

  <h3>Which Method Is Simplest?</h3>
  <p>The best formula is the one that matches the available measurements. If both legs are known, use <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> and skip trigonometry. If a leg, the hypotenuse, and the included angle are known, the calculator's two modes are the direct route. The tangent and double-angle forms are useful for hand calculations with special angles, but adding steps the inputs do not require only adds places for errors to hide.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: How the angle affects the area -->
<section id="sec-angle-effect" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Does the Acute Angle Affect Right Triangle Area?</h2>
  <p>Sine grows as an acute angle increases, so a larger angle seems to promise a larger area. That holds only when the other quantities stay fixed, and in a right triangle the sides and the angle cannot change independently.</p>

  <h3>Fixed Hypotenuse, Changing Angle</h3>
  <p>For a fixed hypotenuse, the area can be written as <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{c^2\\sin(2A)}{4}]]')} />. The factor <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(2A)]]')} /> rises as <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> moves from near <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} /> toward <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />, then falls as <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. The area therefore increases over the first half of the acute range and decreases over the second half. The two halves are mirror images through the complementary-angle relationship <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />: swapping the angles swaps the legs and leaves the area unchanged.</p>

  <h3>Maximum Area for a Fixed Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(2A)\\leq1]]')} />, the largest possible area is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\max}=\\frac{c^2}{4}}]]]')} />, reached when <span set:html={rich('[[A=B=45^\\circ]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> the maximum area is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\max}=25\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. This is the isosceles right triangle of Example 4: equal legs make the product of the legs, and hence the area, as large as the fixed hypotenuse allows.</p>

  <h3>Why Area Does Not Simply Increase With Angle in Every Case</h3>
  <p>A claim about increasing angles is meaningless without naming what is held constant. With a fixed hypotenuse the relationship rises then falls; with a fixed leg the relationship is different again. Treating area as a simple increasing function of angle is a common oversimplification, and this section exists to keep it out of your work.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Units, degrees, rounding -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Area Units, Degrees, and Rounding</h2>

  <h3>Square Metres</h3>
  <p>If the inputs are in metres, the area comes out in square metres: <span set:html={rich('[[4\\text{ m}\\times5\\text{ m}\\times0.6/2=6\\text{ m}^2]]')} />. The multiplication of two lengths always produces a square unit, whatever the unit system.</p>

  <h3>Square Centimetres</h3>
  <p>Centimetre inputs produce square-centimetre areas, as in the kite panel example with its <span set:html={rich('[[150\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> result. Converting between square units squares the linear conversion factor: 1 m&#178; = 10,000 cm&#178;, not 100.</p>

  <h3>Square Feet</h3>
  <p>Foot inputs produce square-foot areas, as in the sail example with its <span set:html={rich('[[30\\text{ ft}^2]]')} /> result. Convert all inputs to one length unit before calculating; the calculator cannot mix metres and feet.</p>

  <h3>Degrees vs. Radians</h3>
  <p>The calculator expects angles in degrees. To convert, use <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Degrees}=\\text{Radians}\\times\\frac{180}{\\pi}]]')} />. For example, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\text{ radians}=30^\\circ]]')} />. Entering a radian value where degrees are expected is one of the most damaging mistakes, because the sine of a small radian number looks plausible while being entirely wrong.</p>

  <h3>Why Sine Has No Units</h3>
  <p>Sine is a ratio of two lengths, so the units cancel and the value is dimensionless. Multiplying two lengths by a dimensionless number leaves exactly the square units the area needs. This is also why the sine value is the same whether the sides were measured in metres or feet, as long as the triangle's shape is the same.</p>

  <h3>Why Intermediate Rounding Matters</h3>
  <p>Rounding an angle or a sine value too early can shift the final area. Keep full precision through the calculation and round only the reported result, to a precision the input measurements support. A measurement given to two significant figures does not earn a six-decimal answer.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Area From an Angle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Using angle B with Mode 1 without changing the sides.</strong> Mode 1 pairs <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> with angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; feeding it <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> computes a different triangle's area.</li>
    <li><strong>Using angle A with Mode 2.</strong> Mode 2 pairs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> with angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. Check the mode selector before entering numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing adjacent and opposite.</strong> These labels depend on the reference angle: fix the angle first, then label the legs relative to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering the hypotenuse into the leg field.</strong> The leg must be shorter than <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />; swapping them breaks every consistency check.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the wrong included angle.</strong> The angle must sit between the two entered sides, or the sine projection is wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting the sine function.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[bc/2]]')} /> alone multiplies two non-perpendicular lengths; the result is not the triangle's area.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to divide by two.</strong> The unhalved product is the parallelogram's area, not the triangle's.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering radians when degrees are expected.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(0.64)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)]]')} /> are entirely different numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering 0 or 90 degrees.</strong> Neither describes a non-degenerate right triangle with these inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering negative or zero lengths.</strong> Lengths are positive; anything else has no geometric meaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering a leg longer than the hypotenuse.</strong> The hypotenuse is always the longest side, so this entry contradicts the setup.</li>
    <li><strong>Using inconsistent inputs.</strong> Check <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\cos(B)]]')} />; a number from mismatched inputs belongs to no right triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing units.</strong> Convert all lengths to one unit first; metres with centimetres scales the answer by 100.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting linear units.</strong> The area unit is the square of the length unit: m&#178;, not m.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding the angle too early.</strong> Round only the final answer; early rounding shifts the sine.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating the modes as interchangeable.</strong> Each mode is its own formula with its own input contract.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Wrong-Versus-Correct Example</h3>
  <p>Take the 3-4-5 triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times\\sin(A))/2\\approx6]]')} />. If the wrong angle is substituted, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times\\sin(B))/2\\approx8]]')} />, not 6. The mistake uses the incorrect included angle for sides <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and 8 is simply the wrong triangle's area.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: Verification methods -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Calculated Area Is Correct</h2>

  <h3>Method 1: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>For a valid right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />. For the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. A failure here means the side inputs describe no right triangle at all.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Check the Legs Formula</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /> independently. For <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=6]]')} />. Agreement with the angle-based result confirms the whole computation.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Check the Complementary Angle</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. The sum must hold for the entered angle to be a genuine acute angle of the triangle.</p>

  <h3>Method 4: Check the Sine Relationship</h3>
  <p>For Mode 1, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} />; for Mode 2, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(B)=b/c]]')} />. In the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)\\approx3/5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(53.13^\\circ)\\approx4/5]]')} />. This is the same relationship the formula uses, checked in the opposite direction.</p>

  <h3>Method 5: Verify the Input Consistency</h3>
  <p>For Mode 1, <span set:html={rich('[[b\\approx c\\cos(A)]]')} />; for Mode 2, <span set:html={rich('[[a\\approx c\\cos(B)]]')} />. The approximation symbol is appropriate when the angle measurement has been rounded.</p>

  <h3>Method 6: Check the Maximum Area</h3>
  <p>For a fixed hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[K\\leq c^2/4]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, no valid area exceeds 6.25 square units. A computed area above that bound signals an input error immediately.</p>

  <h3>Method 7: Check Square Units</h3>
  <p>Ensure the result is expressed in the square of the input length unit. This catches the surprisingly common habit of reporting the area number with the length unit unchanged.</p>
</section>

