<!-- Conclusion -->
<section id="sec-conclusion" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>An Angle, a Leg, and the Hypotenuse Are All You Need</h2>
  <p>The angle-based area method is the base-height rule with the height rebuilt from the hypotenuse. Once the included angle is paired with the correct sides, sine supplies the perpendicular leg, and the area is one substitution away. The two modes are two doors into the same room: Mode 1 enters through angle A, Mode 2 through angle B, and both arrive at the same area.</p>
  <p>The discipline that makes the method trustworthy is consistency: check that the leg, hypotenuse, and angle agree through the cosine relation, verify the area against the legs formula when the second leg can be recovered, and keep the angle-side pairing exact. With those checks in place, the answer earns its trust.</p>
  <p>Use this page's calculator whenever a leg, the hypotenuse, and the included angle are the measured data, and reach for the legs, altitude, or semiperimeter calculators when the measurements point elsewhere.</p>
</section>
